counterexample:
Low-level database engines, operating systems, or game engines where extreme manual hardware tuning is a primary feature; legitimate user-owned high-level policies such as explicit priority classifications passed to a scheduler; and caller requirements such as an explicit latency budget, maximum retry count, or resource limit that encode a required budget. An internal RTT estimator is an example of appropriately internalized tuning, not proof that all deadlines should adapt.
```rust
struct NetworkClient {
    // Automatically estimates the optimal timeout dynamically based on measured RTT
    rtt_estimator: RttEstimator,
}
```

fix:
Keep the public configuration minimal: remove unnecessary micro-tuning and choose sensible, tested, self-tuning defaults. Where the setting really is an internal heuristic, derive it from relevant runtime measurements (e.g., estimating retry intervals from measured round-trip time with a multiplier), pulling complexity downward into the system. Preserve explicit caller-owned policy constraints and justified expert controls; an adaptive mechanism must operate within them rather than replacing them.
```rust
struct NetworkClient {
    rtt_estimator: RttEstimator, // Dynamic policy calculation
}
```
